' A real, ordinary ArcoBASIC program meant to be compiled BY the Fission
' Compiler Substrate's own native x86-64 backend (fission/amir/lower_x86_64.abas)
' -- see fission/tests/native_programs/hello_native.abas's own header
' comment for the directory convention.
'
' The real growable-array representation: Array.Push/Add/Append/Unshift/
' Insert/Extend/Resize. This backend's array/Bytes "value" is now a
' STABLE 2-slot header {length, dataPtr} whose own address never moves
' once allocated, with the actual elements living in a SEPARATE data
' buffer only `dataPtr` points to (see fission/native_runtime/
' host_bridge.c's own header comment on `arco_array_alloc` for the full
' reasoning) -- this is what makes real, correct GROWTH possible while
' preserving the oracle's own true reference semantics: an ALIAS of an
' array (`b = a`) sees a later `Array.Push(a, ...)` immediately, exactly
' like the oracle, confirmed below (`b` prints the pushed element too).
' Growing a single array across many successive Pushes (inside a WHILE
' loop) is also exercised, confirming the header's own address survives
' every reallocation of its own data buffer.
'
' Add/Append are confirmed real ALIASES of Push in the oracle (the exact
' same registered C++ function). Resize is scoped to its real
' 3-argument form only (a real, disclosed, narrower scope cut, matching
' the fixed-arity precedent Bytes.New/Array.New/RANGE/Path.Join already
' established -- the oracle's own 2-argument form defaults `fill` to a
' real NULL).
'
' Expected output verified against legacy `ArcoFission compile-run` on this
' identical source as the equivalence-testing oracle.
a = [1, 2, 3]
b = a
PRINT Array.Push(a, 4)
PRINT a
PRINT b

n = [10]
i = 1
WHILE i < 10
    ignored = Array.Push(n, n[i - 1] + 10)
    i = i + 1
WEND
PRINT n
PRINT LEN(n)

c = [2, 3, 4]
PRINT Array.Unshift(c, 1)
PRINT c

d = [1, 2, 4, 5]
PRINT Array.Insert(d, 2, 3)
PRINT d

e = [1, 2]
PRINT Array.Insert(e, 0, 0)
PRINT e

f = [1, 2]
PRINT Array.Insert(f, 2, 3)
PRINT f

g = [1, 2]
h = [3, 4, 5]
PRINT Array.Extend(g, h)
PRINT g

j = [1, 2, 3]
PRINT Array.Resize(j, 5, 99)
PRINT j
PRINT Array.Resize(j, 2, 0)
PRINT j

k = [1, 2, 3]
PRINT Array.Add(k, 4)
PRINT k
PRINT Array.Append(k, 5)
PRINT k

strs = ["a", "b"]
PRINT Array.Push(strs, "c")
PRINT strs
